\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Hop Powdery Mildew at the Landscape Level}}{1}{section*.1}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{These principles hold across a range of market demand scenarios that impose different crop quality standards, with the optimal number of early-season fungicide applications remaining largely consistent despite substantially different crop prices. The magnitude of profit losses is greatest under high market demand, reflecting higher per-unit crop value and more severe revenue penalties from quality defects.}}{1}{section*.2}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{out-degree centrality}}{3}{section*.3}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Bioeconomic models have been developed for multiple host-pathosystems, with the particular form of the model potentially including social \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {etherton_are_2023, Jeddi_DePaula_2025} or biophysical processes \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {Jeddi_DePaula_2025, Lazebnik} depending on the purpose of the model and specific system. The scale of prediction of bio-economic models varies as well, ranging from individual plants within a field to landscapes \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {donatelli}. Linking epidemiological models to crop damage and economic outcomes when pathogens potentially disperse over long distances is a challenging problem \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {Cunniffe2015}. Modeling dispersal at the mesoscale is especially challenging because microclimate, canopy architecture, and terrain and other landscape features influence aspects of inoculum dispersal, transport, and deposition \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {Aylor2017}. The well known SIR and SEIR frameworks may require extensive adaptation or simply fail to capture the complexity of agricultural systems and unique properties of a given pathosystem \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {Lazebnik}. Furthmore, estimation of $R_0$ to understand epidemic thresholds can be exceedingly difficult in SEIR models because high-quality data are rarely available and numerous assumptions must be made in models that capture the biological complexity of an entire system \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {delamater_2019}. Agent-based process-based models offer an alternative \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {donatelli}, but these models also require many assumptions and carry heavy computational costs and data inputs that may be unavailable \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {Firester2018}. Statistical network spreading models can offer a balance of biological realism and tractability needed to enable prediction of economically optimal outcomes in complex bio-economic systems with non-random mixing of sessile individuals (i.e., fields).}}{4}{section*.4}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{in complex social-ecological systems}}{5}{section*.5}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{.}}{5}{section*.6}%
