\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The mean and variance of the estimated real price per unit for each pesticide are provided in Supplemental Table S2.}}{7}{section*.17}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Profits are defined as revenue minus fixed and variable costs}}{8}{section*.18}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{for a given year}}{8}{section*.19}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{compute the area-weighted mean profit per hectare across all yards, so that larger yards contribute proportionally more to the regional estimate}}{8}{section*.20}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Parameter estimates for the damage functions are given in Supplemental Table \ref {tab:damage_function_parameters}}}{10}{section*.30}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{s solicited from two hop producers and a hop merchant}}{10}{section*.34}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{out-degree centrality}}{11}{section*.38}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{. Out-degree centrality was computed from a directed transmission network: for each ordered pair of yards, the edge weight from source yard $i$ to target yard $j$ is given by $a_i \cdot w_{ij} \cdot (1 + d_{ij})^{-2}$, incorporating source yard area, directional wind run, and a power-law distance decay (cf.\ Equation \ref {eq:1}). The weighted out-degree---the sum of a yard's outgoing edge weights---measures its potential to disperse inoculum to other yards in the landscape}}{11}{section*.39}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{, where degree-centrality weights each yard by the product of yard area, average wind run, and a decreasing function of inter-yard distance as a measure of disease transmission potential}}{11}{section*.40}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{out-degree centrality}}{11}{section*.41}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{(see Appendix \ref {sec:susceptibility_adjustment} for a detailed derivation of this adjustment)}}{12}{section*.42}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In May, the number of fungicide applications for each yard was drawn stochastically. If a yard \textit {did not} contain disease, its spray count was drawn from a Poisson distribution with mean $\lambda _0 = 0.43$, the observed average for disease-free yards in the raw data, representing routine prophylactic spray behavior. If a yard \textit {did} harbor at least one flag shoot (i.e.\ $y_{0,i} > 0$), its spray count was drawn from a Poisson distribution with mean $\lambda _{\text {may}}$, a user-specified parameter ranging from 0 to 10 to represent varying intensities of early-season intervention.}}{12}{section*.43}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{From June onward, the expected number of fungicide applications in each yard was predicted deterministically by Poisson regression models fitted to the grower survey data, with spray counts in each month conditional on disease incidence in the preceding month (Appendix \ref {sec:poisson_regression}). Late-season (post-July) applications were also predicted by the Poisson regression model; these contributed to management costs but did not further affect simulated disease progression.}}{12}{section*.44}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{out-degree centrality}}{12}{section*.45}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{stochastic variability arising from three sources: binomial draws of initial flag shoots ($y_{0,i}$), Poisson draws of May fungicide applications, and random assignment of \textit {V6}-virulent strain presence across yards}}{12}{section*.46}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{disease-free baseline in which yards still receive routine prophylactic fungicide applications but no disease is present}}{12}{section*.47}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{/ Data Source}}{13}{section*.49}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{stochastic variability (see text)}}{13}{section*.50}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{out-degree centrality}}{13}{section*.51}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{quantile}}{13}{section*.52}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Subscripts on parameters index cultivars susceptible only to strains with \textit {V6}-virulence or cultivars susceptible to all strains.}}{15}{section*.58}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{that increases longer distance dispersal}}{15}{section*.59}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The simulation results reveal how profitability depends on initial epidemic conditions, specifically the initial inoculum dose ($p_0$), pathogen diversity (proportion of \textit {V6}-virulent strains), out-degree centrality quantile of the initially infected yards, and market demand (Figure \ref {fig:profit_heatmap_2014}). All simulated scenarios resulted in negative relative profit changes, indicating that any level of disease incidence reduces profit relative to the disease-free baseline. We present results primarily for 2014, the year with the highest observed disease incidence, and note that patterns were qualitatively consistent across all four years of the study period (Supplemental Figures \ref {fig:profit_2015}, \ref {fig:profit_2016}, and \ref {fig:profit_2017}). Across the four years, the maximum profit loss in the worst-case scenario (high demand, 100\% \textit {V6}, highest degree-centrality quantile, no sprays, $p_0 = 10^{-2}$) ranged from $-6.7$\% (2015) to $-10.7$\% (2014).}}{15}{section*.60}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The initial probability of disease ($p_0$) was the dominant factor determining the magnitude of profit loss and the value of fungicide intervention (Figure \ref {fig:profit_heatmap_2014}).}}{16}{section*.61}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{\textbf {Low inoculum ($p_0 \leq 10^{-4}$):} When the initial probability of disease was very low, profit changes were small and insensitive to the number of fungicide sprays, out-degree centrality quantile, or pathogen diversity. In 2014, profit losses at $p_0 = 10^{-5}$ ranged from $-0.1$\% to $-1.1$\% under high demand and remained below $-0.7$\% under low demand, with no systematic response to increasing spray numbers. This indicates that at sufficiently low inoculum levels, the epidemic fails to develop regardless of management inputs, and additional fungicide applications represent unnecessary costs.}}{16}{section*.62}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{\textbf {Moderate to high inoculum ($p_0 \geq 10^{-3}$):} As $p_0$ increased, profitability became sensitive to management decisions. At $p_0 = 10^{-2}$ under high demand with no sprays, profit losses ranged from $-2.9$\% (lowest out-degree centrality quantile, 0\% \textit {V6}) to $-10.7$\% (highest out-degree centrality quantile, 100\% \textit {V6}). Applying fungicides substantially reduced these losses: 5 sprays in May was optimal under high demand for the worst-case scenario, reducing losses from $-10.7$\% to $-4.3$\%. However, additional sprays beyond this optimum were counterproductive---10 sprays yielded a loss of $-5.4$\%, reflecting diminishing disease control benefits offset by rising fungicide costs.}}{16}{section*.63}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The proportion of \textit {V6}-virulent strains in the pathogen population strongly modulated profit outcomes because \textit {V6}-virulent strains can infect cultivars both with and without \textit {R6} resistance, effectively increasing the susceptible host population in the landscape. Under the most severe epidemic scenario ($p_0 = 10^{-2}$, no sprays, highest out-degree centrality quantile, high demand), increasing the \textit {V6}-virulent proportion from 0\% to 100\% more than tripled the profit loss, from $-3.3$\% to $-10.7$\%. The relationship was monotonically increasing: intermediate proportions of 25\%, 50\%, and 75\% \textit {V6} yielded losses of $-6.3$\%, $-7.8$\%, and $-9.2$\%, respectively. This gradient was present across all market demand levels, though attenuated in magnitude under low demand ($-0.7$\% at 0\% \textit {V6} to $-2.2$\% at 100\% \textit {V6}).}}{16}{section*.64}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{out-degree centrality}}{17}{section*.65}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Market demand conditions shifted the magnitude of profit losses and, in some cases, the optimal management strategy. Under the most severe epidemic conditions without sprays, losses declined from $-10.7$\% under high demand to $-4.2$\% under moderate and $-2.2$\% under low demand. At the optimum, 5 sprays minimized losses under high ($-4.3$\%) and moderate ($-1.8$\%) demand, while 4 sprays was sufficient under low demand ($-1.0$\%). The greater sensitivity of profit to disease under high demand reflects the higher per-unit crop value and the consequent larger revenue penalty from quality defects. These patterns were consistent across all four years of the study period (Supplemental Figures \ref {fig:profit_2015}, \ref {fig:profit_2016}, and \ref {fig:profit_2017}).}}{17}{section*.67}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Three key findings emerge from our simulations. First, there exists a critical threshold in initial inoculum pressure (approximately $p_0 = 10^{-4}$) below which economic losses are negligible regardless of management, but above which profitability becomes acutely sensitive to early-season fungicide timing and intensity, suggesting opportunities for area-wide interventions targeting primary inoculum reduction. Second, above this threshold, the economic impact of disease is modulated by the network position of initially infected yards and pathogen diversity, with initial infections in highly connected yards and virulent strains amplifying losses and requiring more intensive intervention. Third, quality-sensitive market conditions sustain near-maximum fungicide intensity even under low demand, with the optimal spray count falling by only one application despite substantially lower crop prices, challenging traditional economic injury level concepts when crop value is tied to quality standards rather than yield alone.}}{18}{section*.68}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In our analysis, under severe epidemic conditions ($p_0 = 10^{-2}$ and 100\% \textit {V6} strain under high market demand), the optimal number of early-season fungicide applications was up to two higher when primary inoculum was concentrated in the most connected yards versus the least connected in the network, and even at the optimal spray level the most connected yards still incurred greater losses ($-4.3$\% vs.\ $-3.1$\%).}}{19}{section*.69}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Notably, a single early-season fungicide application can be counterproductive in this context: it may suppress disease in less-connected yards while providing a false sense of adequate control in high-centrality yards where the epidemic persists through numerous transmission pathways, temporarily widening the disparity in outcomes across the network. Only with sustained fungicide pressure does this disparity narrow, eventually equalizing outcomes across centrality quantiles.}}{19}{section*.70}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Out-degree centrality}}{19}{section*.71}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{}}{20}{section*.72}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Additionally, our optimization is partial: only early-season (May) fungicide applications are treated as decision variables, while later-season sprays follow deterministically from the fitted model based on observed disease state, meaning the full seasonal spray program is not jointly optimized. The proportion of \textit {V6}-virulent strains is also held uniform across all yards within a given simulation run, whereas in practice strain composition likely varies spatially and may shift over the course of a season.}}{21}{section*.73}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Extensions to the model to accommodate more complex pathogen race structure are possible, which may be informative since a third pathogen race has evolved since the original data acquisition \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {Gent2017} . We expect the overall findings of this research will hold across more complex patterns of pathogen virulence conditioned by \textit {R}-genes that provide qualitative resistance.}}{21}{section*.74}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Disease transmission network among sampled hop yards}}{27}{section*.25}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{A representative sample of hop yards (one per out-degree centrality quantile, 2017) connected by directed transmission edges. Node size is proportional to yard area. Green nodes denote cultivars lacking \textit {R6} resistance (susceptible to both strains); blue nodes denote cultivars possessing \textit {R6} (susceptible to \textit {V6}-virulent strains only). Solid gold edges indicate \textit {V6}-virulent source infection; dashed gray edges indicate non-\textit {V6}-virulent source infection. Edge width reflects directed transmission weight (Equation \ref {eq:1}); edges are omitted where strain--cultivar incompatibility precludes transmission.}}{27}{section*.26}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Refer to Section 3 for the definition of out-degree centrality and the quantile classification used.}}{27}{section*.27}%
